#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""`admin`: the maintainers' one command on the gateway (#741). A maintainer SSHes in and runs
`sudo admin <task>`; nothing here runs from a laptop. gateway-setup.sh installs it as
/usr/local/sbin/admin, beside the Brief relay whose Gateway Key functions it uses.

The tasks that touch a Household run the shared program (ADR 0035) as the sign-up service's user,
as signup.sudoers lets it, but for `household`, which runs it as that Household's own user
(#782); the Gateway Key tasks call Bifrost's admin API on localhost, with the
admin login read from bifrost.env, never the provider key beside it. It prints only to the
terminal and writes no log of its own; no key's value is printed except by `key new`. `deploy`
(ADR 0036) snapshots the server's state, fetches a commit of the public repo and runs that
commit's gateway-setup.sh with it, and notes each deploy in its record, which `households` reads
to say which commit runs; `rollback` runs the deploy before's gateway-setup.sh again, and with
--restore first brings back the snapshot taken before the deploy it leaves.

`check --mail` is the nightly check (#745), which parent-recap-check.timer runs after the evening
runs: the Households' status, the gateway's services and a pending reboot, mailed to the
maintainers from the Brief's address through Email Delivery, as the relay sends, only when
something is wrong. The mail holds only the status lines of the Households marked ❌, ⚠️ or ❓,
the services' names and the reboot. `signed-up LABEL` (#814), which the sign-up service runs
through sudo once it has made a Household, mails them the same way that Household's label, its
note in households.json, with the name the family gave, and the time its Brief comes: nothing else
of it.

Python's standard library only, so it runs on the host's own python3.
"""
